The CSS issue is fixed — build compiles cleanly with no warnings. The port issue is just another dev server instance running. 

The root cause was: Tailwind v4 was scanning the entire project directory by default, including `logs/pre_tool_use.json` (which logged previous fix attempts containing `font-[family-name:var()]` and `--font-*`) and `CLAUDE.md` (line 380 had `font-[family-name:var()]`). The fix was `source("../")` in the `@import` directive to restrict scanning to the `src/` directory only.

Here's the final state of `src/app/globals.css`:

```css
@import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Noto+Sans+KR...");
@import "tailwindcss" source("../");
...
```

The Google Fonts `@import url()` is now correctly placed before the Tailwind import (fixing warning #3), and Tailwind only scans `src/` (fixing warnings #1 and #2).

**Production mode.**

Test mode allows public read/write for 30 days then locks down — it's a trap. Production mode starts with locked-down rules, and you set them explicitly.

For image serving you want public read (anyone can view images) but no public write. Set this rule after bucket creation:

```
rules_version = '2';
service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read;
      allow write: if false;
    }
  }
}
```

This makes images publicly accessible via URL (needed for your site) while blocking any uploads except through the Firebase Admin SDK (which you'll use for the migration script).
